<!--
  The drug-order arrangements the standard test dataset does not carry. The first three are issue
  #317's; the rest are issue #315's — the one a stop-date reading must not invent a date for, the
  live one it must not invent a date FOR EITHER, and a same-date pair that makes an ordering rule
  discriminable.

  1. ORDER 9317 — an order that lapsed by its DURATION: action NEW, auto_expire_date in the past,
     date_stopped NULL. querystore's DrugOrderRecordSerializer renders ". Stopped: <date>" for
     getDateStopped() and ". Action: DISCONTINUE" for the action, and renders NOTHING for
     auto_expire_date — measured by running it, and pinned by
     QuerystoreOrderTextMarkerTest.anAutoExpireDateAloneIsNotVisibleInTheRenderedText. So this
     order's rendered text is byte-shaped exactly like a live order's while OrderService excludes it
     from getActiveOrders. That gap is the ticket. The standard dataset's only auto-expired drug
     orders (22 and 44) also carry order_action DISCONTINUE, so their text DOES say they ended and
     they cannot exercise it.

     Deliberately the same drug and concept as standard-dataset order 3 (drug 2 / concept 792,
     Triomune-30, patient 2, live), which makes patient 2's chart the renewal shape — one live record
     and one lapsed record naming the same drug.

  2. ORDER 9318 — a patient whose ONLY drug order has ended, so their active-order set is EMPTY.
     That emptiness is an ANSWER (every record is marked not-active), and it must never be confused
     with the order read having FAILED, which is the fail-closed hazard the ticket names. It is also
     the arrangement issue #315 reported. Patients 2 and 7 both have live
     orders, so neither can reach this state; patient 6 of the standard dataset has no orders and no
     encounter, so both are supplied.

  Row order matters: dbunit inserts tables in the order they first appear, so the encounter must
  precede the orders that reference it, and rows of one table are kept contiguous. Orders 9317 and
  9318 carry the same attribute set for the same reason; 9319 deliberately adds date_stopped and
  9320 deliberately omits both end dates, each of which is the whole point of that row.

  A separate file rather than rows added to ChartSearchAiTestData.xml: that file carries no order
  rows at all and is loaded by HibernateChartSearchAiDAOTest, whose dataset must not change
  underneath it.
-->
<dataset>
	<encounter encounter_id="9318" encounter_type="1" patient_id="6" location_id="2" form_id="1"
	           encounter_datetime="2008-08-19 00:00:00.0" creator="1"
	           date_created="2008-08-19 12:34:40.0" voided="false"
	           uuid="9318aaaa-0000-4000-8000-00000000318a" />

	<orders order_id="9317" order_type_id="1" order_number="ORD-9317" urgency="ROUTINE"
	        order_action="NEW" concept_id="792" orderer="1" instructions="1x daily"
	        date_activated="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" auto_expire_date="2008-01-08 00:00:00.0"
	        creator="1" date_created="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" voided="false" patient_id="2"
	        uuid="9317aaaa-0000-4000-8000-00000000317a" care_setting="1" encounter_id="6" />
	<orders order_id="9318" order_type_id="1" order_number="ORD-9318" urgency="ROUTINE"
	        order_action="NEW" concept_id="792" orderer="1" instructions="1x daily"
	        date_activated="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" auto_expire_date="2008-01-09 00:00:00.0"
	        creator="1" date_created="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" voided="false" patient_id="6"
	        uuid="9318bbbb-0000-4000-8000-00000000318b" care_setting="1" encounter_id="9318" />
	<!--
	  3. ORDER 9319 — an order core cannot evaluate: date_stopped AFTER auto_expire_date, both past.
	     OrderValidator checks date_activated against each of those two dates and never checks them
	     against EACH OTHER, and OrderServiceImpl.stopOrder writes exactly this row through the public
	     API when the supported global property order.allowSettingStopDateOnInactiveOrders is on. So
	     the shape is reachable rather than merely malformed.

	     Order.isDiscontinued and Order.isExpired both THROW APIException on it, before any other
	     test, while the SQL getActiveOrders applies simply answers. It belongs to patient 2 and does
	     two jobs. Charted, its own record must say NOTHING rather than "not active" — the module may
	     not report as ended a prescription it could not evaluate. Uncharted, which is how every other
	     case in the file sees it, it must not be able to take the mark off the records that ARE
	     charted.
	-->
	<orders order_id="9319" order_type_id="1" order_number="ORD-9319" urgency="ROUTINE"
	        order_action="NEW" concept_id="792" orderer="1" instructions="1x daily"
	        date_activated="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" auto_expire_date="2008-01-05 00:00:00.0"
	        date_stopped="2008-01-10 00:00:00.0"
	        creator="1" date_created="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" voided="false" patient_id="2"
	        uuid="9319cccc-0000-4000-8000-00000000319c" care_setting="1" encounter_id="6" />
	<!--
	  4. ORDER 9320 — a DISCONTINUE order carrying NEITHER date_stopped NOR auto_expire_date, which
	     is what DrugOrder.cloneForDiscontinuing() produces: it sets the action, care setting,
	     concept, drug, free-text drug, order type, patient and previous order, and neither end date.
	     Order.isActive() answers false on it from the action alone, before any date is consulted, so
	     the order is not in force AND core publishes no effective stop date for it. That pair is the
	     cell issue #315's statement turns on, and the standard dataset cannot reach it: its only
	     DISCONTINUE drug orders, 22 and 44, each carry an auto_expire_date equal to their own
	     date_activated, so getEffectiveStopDate() answers a date there.

	     The instant this discontinuation took effect lives on getPreviousOrder(), not here, and the
	     module deliberately does not derive it — see ADR Decision 97. Order 222's own date_stopped is
	     left NULL on purpose: core's Order.isActive() reads an order's own fields and never walks
	     forward to a discontinuing sibling, so pointing at 222 cannot move any other case in this
	     file, and the tests assert both halves of the shape off core rather than assuming them.
	-->
	<orders order_id="9320" order_type_id="1" order_number="ORD-9320" urgency="ROUTINE"
	        order_action="DISCONTINUE" concept_id="792" orderer="1" previous_order_id="222"
	        date_activated="2008-01-11 00:00:00.0"
	        creator="1" date_created="2008-01-11 00:00:00.0" voided="false" patient_id="2"
	        uuid="9320dddd-0000-4000-8000-00000000320d" care_setting="1" encounter_id="6" />

	<!--
	  5. ORDER 9321 — a LIVE duration-based prescription: action NEW, date_activated past,
	     auto_expire_date in the FUTURE, date_stopped NULL. Order.isActive() is TRUE for it, and
	     getEffectiveStopDate() still answers that future date, because that accessor is
	     dateStopped-else-autoExpireDate and knows nothing about whether the date has passed.

	     That pair is what witnesses the contract in SerializedRecord.orderStopDate — non-null implies
	     the currency mark is FALSE. Without it, the two guards that enforce it (the forRecord gate in
	     stopDateForRecord and the else-narrowing in readingOf) can BOTH be removed with the suite
	     green, and the module then publishes a stop date for a live prescription. Found by review.

	     Deliberately the commonest live shape rather than an exotic one: core sets auto_expire_date
	     from an order's duration, so this is what most running prescriptions look like. Standard
	     order 3, which the live cases used before, carries NEITHER end date — so no arrangement of
	     either guard could have produced a date for it, and asserting "no stop date" there asserted
	     nothing.

	  6. ORDER 9322 — a second ENDED order sharing order 9317's date_activated, so the two chart
	     records carry the SAME date. extractCitedReferences sorts the citations by record date, and
	     on a chart assembled most-recent-first that sort leaves them in index order whatever the
	     answer printed — so a pair with distinct dates cannot tell "ordered by citation index" from
	     "ordered as the resolution handed them over". With the dates equal the sort is a no-op and
	     the resolution's order follows the answer's markers, which is what makes the ordering rule
	     in ChartSearchAiUtils.orderStopDates discriminable. Also found by review.
	-->
	<orders order_id="9321" order_type_id="1" order_number="ORD-9321" urgency="ROUTINE"
	        order_action="NEW" concept_id="792" orderer="1" instructions="1x daily"
	        date_activated="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" auto_expire_date="2099-01-08 00:00:00.0"
	        creator="1" date_created="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" voided="false" patient_id="2"
	        uuid="9321eeee-0000-4000-8000-00000000321e" care_setting="1" encounter_id="6" />
	<orders order_id="9322" order_type_id="1" order_number="ORD-9322" urgency="ROUTINE"
	        order_action="NEW" concept_id="792" orderer="1" instructions="1x daily"
	        date_activated="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" auto_expire_date="2008-01-07 00:00:00.0"
	        creator="1" date_created="2008-01-01 00:00:00.0" voided="false" patient_id="2"
	        uuid="9322ffff-0000-4000-8000-00000000322f" care_setting="1" encounter_id="6" />

	<drug_order order_id="9317" drug_inventory_id="2" dose="1.0" dose_units="51" as_needed="false"
	            frequency="1" dosing_type="org.openmrs.SimpleDosingInstructions" route="22"
	            quantity="1.0" quantity_units="51" num_refills="0" dispense_as_written="0" />
	<drug_order order_id="9318" drug_inventory_id="2" dose="1.0" dose_units="51" as_needed="false"
	            frequency="1" dosing_type="org.openmrs.SimpleDosingInstructions" route="22"
	            quantity="1.0" quantity_units="51" num_refills="0" dispense_as_written="0" />
	<drug_order order_id="9319" drug_inventory_id="2" dose="1.0" dose_units="51" as_needed="false"
	            frequency="1" dosing_type="org.openmrs.SimpleDosingInstructions" route="22"
	            quantity="1.0" quantity_units="51" num_refills="0" dispense_as_written="0" />
	<drug_order order_id="9320" drug_inventory_id="2" as_needed="0" dispense_as_written="0" />
	<drug_order order_id="9321" drug_inventory_id="2" dose="1.0" dose_units="51" as_needed="false"
	            frequency="1" dosing_type="org.openmrs.SimpleDosingInstructions" route="22"
	            quantity="1.0" quantity_units="51" num_refills="0" dispense_as_written="0" />
	<drug_order order_id="9322" drug_inventory_id="2" dose="1.0" dose_units="51" as_needed="false"
	            frequency="1" dosing_type="org.openmrs.SimpleDosingInstructions" route="22"
	            quantity="1.0" quantity_units="51" num_refills="0" dispense_as_written="0" />
</dataset>
